title: Moving off NixOS and Docker means installing the tools by hand; one script calls Docker, two other things only mention it, and nothing in the server, clients or tests needs it.
pid: 49d94d7f-423f-4835-a238-2c9e891d697b
body: |2-
  The container's image, built with Nix outside this repository,
  supplies everything (the list is for the install script, not for
  you): Rust 1.99 (the code needs edition 2024), cargo-nextest and
  cargo-watch, gcc, cmake and pkg-config for the C libraries cargo
  compiles (libgit2, zlib, zstd), Emacs 31 with magit, Neovim 0.12 with
  nvim-orgmode, its tree-sitter parser, plenary, neogit and diffview,
  Python 3, Node for Claude Code, and git, ripgrep, fd and jq. On a
  plain Linux these come from the package manager, rustup and Neovim's
  plugin manager; the Neovim parser is the part most likely to take an
  afternoon. Claude can write the install script and test it on a fresh
  machine in about half a day (an estimate).
  What names Docker: 'bash/import-org-roam.sh' runs its import with
  'docker exec'; the 'windows/' folder packages skg for Windows users
  in Docker, a separate product; and 'coding-advice/docker.org' tells
  Claude it is in a container. The image also points Claude's settings
  at a folder in the repository, so sessions survive a rebuild; without
  it the team's tools fall back to '~/.claude' and still work.
  If you do not answer, nothing moves.
